Ladybird gives each extension one world per document: a realm whose
global is another wrapper of the same Window, with an Extension
WrapperWorld behind it, and installs “browser” and “chrome” (one
object) on that global. WebKit creates one DOMWrapperWorld per
extension, named “WebExtension-<id>”, and WebExtensionControllerProxy::
globalObjectIsAvailableForFrame() sets both “browser” and “chrome” on
the world’s global to one WebExtensionAPINamespace when the frame’s
window object becomes available in that world. Blink allocates a
Blink isolated world id per extension in IsolatedWorldManager::
GetOrCreateIsolatedWorldForHost(), and the renderer’s Dispatcher::
DidCreateScriptContext() registers a ScriptContext of type
kContentScript whose NativeExtensionBindingsSystem installs only
“chrome”. Gecko runs a content script in a Cu.Sandbox whose
sandboxPrototype is the content window, with wantXrays, and
ContentScriptContextChild defines both “browser” and “chrome” through
Schemas.exportLazyGetter. Ladybird keeps the listeners of an API
event in a GC cell the world owns, which is the role of WebKit’s
WebExtensionAPIEvent listener list and Blink’s per-context
APIEventHandler.
